[Music]  welcome back and get ready state leaders  say election night could look  more like an election week or even an  election  month and the controversy surrounding  the u.s postal service right now that is  only  one factor among many it will also take  time to deal with different state laws  and to simply open all the ballots that  are expected to be  mailed in plus leaders want to make sure  they get it right that's always  important  cbs news chief washington correspondent  major garrett joins us he's been looking  into all of this  major good morning you spoke to a  bipartisan group of  secretaries of state these are the  people in charge of elections at the  state level  also an election security official what  do they tell you about how they're  preparing  that this is going to take a while that  because the nation in some areas is  going through a rather large transition  from in-person to mail-in voting this  process will play out for a longer  period of time especially where counting  is concerned here's the good news though  states that have a long history of  voting by mail are giving all the advice  they can to states that are trying to  make this  transition and where the learning curve  is somewhat steep  they also tell us tony that the  president's rhetoric about rigging and  fraud does not help them  it confuses and sows doubts among voters  but they're working together  persistently trying to make sure this  election  is believable trusted for the losers  and the winners when new york city  voters cast primary ballots in june  few expected this would be the scene at  the board of elections  no declared winner one month later  president trump cried fraud  without evidence i think you should redo  that election  it has taken election officials some  time to do the counting  about 40 percent of new york state's  primary ballots arrived by mail  a nearly 11-fold increase over  pre-pandemic levels  now new york and other states are  scrambling to scale up mail-in voting  more ballots by mail means results could  be slower  and that means voters will need to be  patient absentee ballots or mail-in  ballots  tend to be more labor-intensive because  you have to  check the signature on the the envelope  to match it against the voter  registration record you need to  open the envelope separate out the  security envelope  then remove the voter's ballot kim wyman  is washington's republican secretary of  state  we want accuracy above everything so  it's going to take time  washington is among five states that  already vote almost exclusively by  mail this fall in an effort to keep  voters safe from coronavirus five  more states and washington d.c will  automatically send  mail-in ballots to all registered voters  well the problem with the mail in voting  number one you're never going to know  when the election's over  waiting it may be the hardest part but  this year it won't necessarily mean  something's gone wrong  we may not have official results for  quite some time chris krebs oversees  election cyber security  at the department of homeland security  certain states might not get called for  some time  and that's okay be prepared be patient  and most importantly participate in the  process  colorado is an all vote by mail state  where democrat jenna griswold  is the secretary of state it does take a  little longer to process mail ballots  and by the way they're more secure than  a lot of other forms of voting  one thing colorado does to speed things  up it processes mail-in balance before  election day  no results are reported until that night  but griswold says  there are other advantages increases  security  because russia cannot hack a mail ballot  and it's the best way to vote in social  distance  michigan wisconsin and pennsylvania are  all expecting large increases in mail-in  voting this year  and all three were pivotal to deciding  the outcome in 2016  but none allows for early processing of  mail-in ballots  the other states have already figured  this out so i'm hopeful we'll be able to  do so  come november jocelyn benson is  michigan's democratic secretary of state  she has sparred with the gop-controlled  legislature  on this issue not if the legislature  fails to act citizens not just in  michigan but around the country need to  know  exactly why it will take a little bit  longer perhaps even a few days longer to  get the full results out of michigan  again washington secretary of state kim  wyman half of the country believes joe  biden's going to win  half of the country believes president  trump's going to win and our job as  election officials is to inspire  confidence  in the losers so i know the big question  out there how long is it going to take  well if this election is close tony  secretary wyman said the results the  official results may not be known until  mid  to late november and as a part of that  we talked a lot of these secretaries of  state they say that among them there is  a prayer  let it be a landslide not just this  election but any election  yeah a lot of politicians are calling  for that as well two important points  that i think i would leave people with  first the number one reason a ballot is  rejected is not that it's late but  because people forget to sign the ballot  or they sign it differently than what's  on their license so sign it and take  your time doing it  second point is if you're not trusting  the postal service at this moment and  you know who could blame you uh  almost all states are supposed to allow  mail-in ballots to be hand-delivered at  local election offices so that's  something people can explore  in their local areas such an important  story major thank you very much

